question.:question/worked-solution:
Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
4 x+\left(8 y\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\
\left(8 y\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\left(4 x\right) \\
\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\frac{4 x}{8 y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope at the given point:

$$
m_{\mathrm{t}}=-\frac{-12}{-8}=- \frac{3}{2}
$$

The tangent line uses $m=- \frac{3}{2}$.

Use the given point and this slope:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-\left(-1\right)&=- \frac{3}{2}\left(x-\left(-3\right)\right) \\
y&=- \frac{3}{2}\left(x-\left(-3\right)\right)+\left(-1\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$. Substitute and simplify:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=- \frac{3}{2}\left(0-\left(-3\right)\right)+\left(-1\right) \\
y&=- \frac{11}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore $P=\left(0,- \frac{11}{2}\right)$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
6 x+\left(4 y\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\
\left(4 y\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\left(6 x\right) \\
\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\frac{6 x}{4 y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope at the given point:

$$
m_{\mathrm{t}}=-\frac{-12}{-8}=- \frac{3}{2}
$$

The tangent line uses $m=- \frac{3}{2}$.

Use the given point and this slope:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-\left(-2\right)&=- \frac{3}{2}\left(x-\left(-2\right)\right) \\
y&=- \frac{3}{2}\left(x-\left(-2\right)\right)+\left(-2\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$. Substitute and simplify:

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=- \frac{3}{2}\left(x-\left(-2\right)\right)+\left(-2\right) \\
x-\left(-2\right)&=-\frac{-2}{- \frac{3}{2}} \\
x&=- \frac{10}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore $P=\left(- \frac{10}{3},0\right)$.
